WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.620
Most advocates show up at the hearing, which is the moment of least influence.

2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:12.436
By the time the agenda packet is posted, staff's recommendation is set, and you are asking elected officials to break from it.

3
00:00:12.436 --> 00:00:15.033
The highest-leverage moments come weeks earlier.

4
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:16.520
Think of it in five steps.

5
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:24.355
First, talk to the project planner or department head while the item is still being framed, and ask what would change the recommendation.

6
00:00:24.355 --> 00:00:29.273
Second, before the staff report is final, send a dated letter with a specific request.

7
00:00:29.273 --> 00:00:38.080
Third, meet decision-makers one at a time, and never carry one member's position to another, because that is how advocates create illegal serial meetings.

8
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.540
Fourth, give public comment that adds something new, and follow up in writing.

9
00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.514
Fifth, after the decision, watch the follow-through.

10
00:00:45.514 --> 00:00:47.973
The research on public comment is sobering.

11
00:00:47.973 --> 00:00:58.496
A study of thousands of comments at Massachusetts planning meetings found that commenters were older, more often homeowners, and far more opposed to new housing than voters as a whole.

12
00:00:58.496 --> 00:01:07.017
Decision-makers know the room is unrepresentative, which is exactly why organized testimony from renters, young families, and workers carries weight.

13
00:01:07.017 --> 00:01:11.498
If an issue might end up in court, raise it before the decision, in writing.

14
00:01:11.498 --> 00:01:20.932
A lawsuit over a land use decision is limited to issues raised at the hearing or in letters delivered before it, and environmental lawsuits have a similar rule.

15
00:01:20.932 --> 00:01:26.180
And the Public Records Act gives you documents within ten days, plus one short extension.

16
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.246
Name the department, a date range, and search terms.

17
00:01:29.246 --> 00:01:32.439
Running for office in Torrance starts with the calendar.

18
00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:35.859
Council Districts 2, 4, and 6 are up on March seventh, 2028.

19
00:01:35.859 --> 00:01:39.052
The mayor and Districts 1, 3, and 5 are up in June 2030.

20
00:01:39.052 --> 00:01:41.960
School board Areas A and B are up in November 2028.

21
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.525
City races file with the Torrance City Clerk.

22
00:01:44.525 --> 00:01:48.060
School board races file with the Los Angeles County Registrar.

23
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:55.757
Before you raise or spend a dollar, file a statement of intention with the state's campaign agency, and open one campaign bank account.

24
00:01:55.757 --> 00:02:00.717
Once you raise or spend two thousand dollars, you register a committee within ten days.

25
00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:06.760
Then come regular reports, plus twenty-four hour reports for large contributions in the final ninety days.

26
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:15.996
Torrance sets its own contribution limit: one thousand dollars per person per candidate per election cycle, with your own loans capped at thirty thousand dollars.

27
00:02:15.996 --> 00:02:18.219
School board races have no state limit.

28
00:02:18.219 --> 00:02:19.815
And remember the Levine Act.

29
00:02:19.815 --> 00:02:31.845
If you run from an advisory commission, you are not restricted during the campaign, but once seated you must step aside on council matters whose parties gave you more than five hundred dollars in the prior year.

30
00:02:31.845 --> 00:02:35.323
Screen your donors against council agendas before you accept.

31
00:02:35.323 --> 00:02:38.173
Finally, keep the campaign separate from the city.

32
00:02:38.173 --> 00:02:41.537
No city staff, email, phones, or offices for campaign work.

33
00:02:41.537 --> 00:02:42.641
The scale is small.

34
00:02:42.641 --> 00:02:48.450
The 2026 mayor's race was decided by about twelve hundred votes out of roughly forty-three thousand.

35
00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.238
A council district is about a sixth of the city.

36
00:02:51.238 --> 00:02:59.023
Races that size reward direct voter contact: knocking on doors, neighborhood coffees, and endorsements from people who know your work.

37
00:02:59.023 --> 00:03:04.367
For more, see the advocacy and running for office sections of the Local Official's Handbook.
